model.en:
Use the promise form of pipeline and one try/catch:

import { pipeline } from 'node:stream/promises';

async function handler(req, res) {
  try {
    await pipeline(
      req,                 // Readable: inbound socket
      zlib.createGunzip(),  // Transform: throws on a malformed body
      createNdjsonParser(), // Transform
      saveSink(),           // Writable
    );
    res.end('ok');
  } catch (err) {
    // gunzip's error lands here; req, gunzip, parser, sink are ALL destroyed
    res.statusCode = 400;
    res.end('bad body');
  }
}

Why it's right: pipeline forwards an error from ANY stage to the single await/catch, and destroys every stream in the chain on both failure and completion — so a malformed body rejects cleanly, req's fd is released, and there is no leak and no uncaughtException. The callback form pipeline(req, gunzip, parser, sink, (err) => …) gives identical guarantees outside an async function. Adding gunzip.on('error') handlers to the old .pipe() chain would stop the crash but NOT release req — you'd still have to manually destroy every stream in the right order, which is exactly what pipeline does for free.

